  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracts in Kentucky totaled $8.1B in FY2026 to date. Kentucky had 76,201 awards during the period, with an average award size of $106K. Kentucky obligations are down 22.5% year over year. The Department of Defense leads buying activity, obligating $7.4B in FY2026 to date. HUMANA GOVERNMENT BUSINESS INC is the largest vendor by dollars, receiving $5.5B in the same period. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest industry group by dollars, with $5.5B. Yearly totals show a decline from prior fiscal years: FY25 obligations were $10.4B and FY24 obligations were $12.3B. The rest of the top buyers include the Department of the Treasury at $183M, the Department of Justice at $147M, and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $136M. The top vendor list beyond the lead includes LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $543M and THALLE - BAUER LLC at $345M. This snapshot covers contract obligations only; FY2026 to date totals are partial and reflect work so far in the fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Kentucky federal contracting changed recently?",
        "body": "Kentucky obligations are down 22.5% year over year. FY26 obligations stand at $8.1B, down from FY25 obligations of $10.4B and FY24 obligations of $12.3B. The number of awards during FY2026 to date is 76,201 with an average award size of $106K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who dominates federal buying and which industries receive the most?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $7.4B in FY2026 to date. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the top industry by dollars, with $5.5B, followed by All Other Support Services at $545M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $368M."
      }
    ]
  },
